dict(id='M57', act=10, set='apartment14', vis='Milo\'s small flat, snow outside. A calendar with a $100 coin on the 1st of every month. A price line wobbles: $10, $8, $5, $8, $10. Shares pile up bigger in the cheap months.',
  lines=[('N', "December, and the three least exciting words in the phrasebook turn out to be the strongest. Dollar cost averaging means investing the same amount on a schedule, whatever the price. Milo puts in a hundred dollars a month. When prices are low, his hundred buys more shares."),
         ('LEXI', "Five months, prices of ten, eight, five, eight and ten. Average price eight twenty. But Milo's average cost is about seven sixty nine, because he bought more when it was cheap."),
         ('MILO', "I did nothing clever and I still won?"), ('LEXI', "That's the point.")]),
dict(id='M58', act=10, set='apartment14', vis='A snowball rolling downhill, picking up snow (coins) and growing huge. Labels: $36,000 PUT IN -> ~$122,000 AFTER 30 YEARS (at 7% a year, not guaranteed).',
  lines=[('N', "Compound interest means earning returns on your returns. Your money grows, then that growth grows too. A hundred dollars a month for thirty years is thirty six thousand dollars put in. At seven percent a year, which is an assumption and never guaranteed, it could grow to about a hundred and twenty two thousand."),
         ('PIP', "Where did the other eighty six thousand come from?"), ('LEXI', "Time.")]),
dict(id='M59', act=10, set='apartment14', vis='A shopping bag full of groceries slowly shrinking in Milo\'s hand as a calendar flips ten years. A price tag melts: $100 -> BUYS $74.',
  lines=[('N', "And the third word is the one working against you. Inflation is prices rising over time, so each dollar buys less. At three percent a year, a hundred dollars buys only about seventy four dollars of today's stuff after ten years. Cash under the mattress quietly shrinks. That's why people invest in the first place.")]),
# ---------------- ACT 11: YOUR WALLET + ENDING ----------------
dict(id='M60', act=11, card="Your Wallet"),
dict(id='M61', act=11, set='wallet14', vis='Stromi the mascot at a little desk with Milo and Lexi. Five stamps slide in: a slice, a basket, a lock with a key, a toolbox with a red X, a snowball.',
  lines=[('N', "So what does this mean for your wallet? This isn't financial advice, just the five ideas Milo kept from his phrasebook."),
         ('N', "One. A share is a slice of a real business, so read the label before you buy. Two. A basket, like an index fund, spreads your risk. Three. Use limit orders when you care about the price. Four. Leverage, shorting and options are sharp tools: learn them before you ever touch them. And five. Invest a steady amount, and let compounding do the heavy lifting."),
         ('MILO', "None of that needed me to sound like Benny."), ('N', "No. It needed you to understand Benny.")]),
dict(id='M62', act=11, set='border14', vis='One year later. Same foggy border booth. Marge flips through Milo\'s phrasebook, now fat with stamps: 75. Lexi sits proudly on Milo\'s shoulder.',
  lines=[('MARGE', "Purpose of visit?"), ('MILO', "Residency.")]),
dict(id='M63', act=11, set='depot14', vis='Back at the courier depot at dawn. Benny leans on the same bike. As Milo speaks, each of the seven jargon tags from the opening flips over to show its meaning in plain words.',
  lines=[('BENNY', "So I went long on a blue chip, got shaken out on a dead cat bounce, shorted it, and got caught in a squeeze."),
         ('MILO', "So you bought a big safe company, your stop loss sold you in a fake recovery, then you bet against it, it jumped, and you had to buy back at the top."),
         ('BENNY', "...When you say it like that, it sounds bad."), ('MILO', "It is bad, Benny."),
         ('N', "Milo still has his five hundred dollars invested, plus a hundred more every month. He never did day trade. But now, when the market talks, he understands every word.")]),
dict(id='M64', act=11, card="Watch next: turning savings into investing", sub="Video 10: the step-by-step path from a savings account to your first investment. General education only, not financial advice. Companies and tickers shown are examples, not recommendations. Figures as of November 2026."),
